# The secure element only answers to the configuration it was formatted
# against. diting ships as two japanese handsets - A201XM (SoftBank) and
# 22081212R (open market) - and their sets differ in the chip issuer id, the
# carrier id and the model string, so one cannot stand in for the other.
#
# Nothing tells the two apart. Both stock firmwares report the same
# androidboot.hardware.sku (diting or ditingp, which is the CN/global panel
# split) and the same fastboot product, and the model number lives only in
# /odm/etc/<sku>_build.prop, which this ROM writes itself. docs/felica.md has
# the comparison of the two dumps.
#
# What the bootloader does say is whether this is a japanese handset at all:
# ro.boot.hwc is JP on one, where a chinese unit says CN and a global one GL
# (measured on a 22081212R, hwc=JP hardware.sku=ditingp hwlevel=MP). So bind
# the open market set on every japanese handset and leave FelicaParts to move
# it, rather than making the common case wait for a choice.
#
# The NFC configuration moves with it. Type F only reaches the element when the
# routing says so - stock japanese firmware sets DEFAULT_SYS_CODE_ROUTE and the
# other route ids to 0x01 (eSE) where the global one leaves them on the UICC -
# and the RF table is tuned per handset. A CN or global handset never enters
# either block and keeps everything as it was extracted.
on property:ro.boot.hwc=JP
    mount none /vendor/etc/felica_22081212R /vendor/etc/felica bind
    mount none /vendor/etc/nfc_22081212R/libnfc-nxp.conf /vendor/etc/libnfc-nxp.conf bind
    mount none /vendor/etc/nfc_22081212R/libnfc-nxp_RF.conf /vendor/libnfc-nxp_RF.conf bind

# Whatever FelicaParts was told to use, bound on top of the above - for a
# handset whose element was provisioned as the other model. This is the only
# way to reach the A201XM set, and it has to stay: the two cannot be told apart
# from the outside, so the one case the default gets wrong needs a way out.
#
# Lives on system_ext because the property has to be writable by the settings
# app: /vendor and /odm scripts expand ${} through vendor_init, which cannot
# read a system property, and a vendor property cannot be set from an app at
# all. init parses this one itself, so it can read either.
#
# Fires when the persistent properties are loaded, which is after the block
# above - ro.boot.hwc is there from the first property load. The NFC HAL starts
# before both on early_hal, but only reads its configuration when the framework
# first opens it, which is far later. A bind mount is invisible to a file
# descriptor somebody already holds, which is the other half of why the settings
# screen asks for a reboot. A value with no directory of its own leaves the
# japanese default in place; init ignores ENOENT.
on property:persist.felica.model=*
    mount none /vendor/etc/felica_${persist.felica.model} /vendor/etc/felica bind
    mount none /vendor/etc/nfc_${persist.felica.model}/libnfc-nxp.conf /vendor/etc/libnfc-nxp.conf bind
    mount none /vendor/etc/nfc_${persist.felica.model}/libnfc-nxp_RF.conf /vendor/libnfc-nxp_RF.conf bind
